      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "First, we need to find the total weight Terrell lifts with the 20-pound weights. We know that each lift is 20 pounds, so with 12 lifts, the total weight is 20 pounds x 12 lifts = 240 pounds.\nNow we need to find out how many times to lift the 15-pound weights in order to lift 240 pounds. We know that each lift is 15 pounds, so we can set up a proportion to solve for the number of lifts, which we'll call x:\n20 pounds / 12 lifts = 15 pounds / x lifts\nCross multiplying the equation, we get 20 pounds x x lifts = 15 pounds x 12 lifts\nSimplifying, we get 20x = 180\nDividing both sides by 20, we get x = 9\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights 9 times in order to lift the same total weight as he did with the 20-pound weights.",
