      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as he does with the 20-pound weights, we need to calculate the total weight he lifts with the 20-pound weights and then find out how many sets of 15-pound weights are needed to lift the same total weight.\n\nLet's denote the number of times Terrell lifts the 15-pound weights as \\( x \\).\n\nThe total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n\\text{Total weight with 20-pound weights} = 2 \\times 20 \\text{ pounds} \\times 12 = 480 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nThe total weight lifted with the 15-pound weights is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total weight with 15-pound weights} = 2 \\times 15 \\text{ pounds} \\times x = 30x \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nWe need the total weight lifted with the 15-pound weights to be equal to the total weight lifted with the 20-pound weights, so we set up the equation:\n\\[\n30x = 480\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we solve for \\( x \\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 30:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{480}{30} = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight.",
